    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border-top: 3px solid #C7922C; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128214; TODAY'S FOCUS</div>
        <div style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 10px;">Slavery ended in 1865. The next fight was over what freedom would mean.</div>
        <div style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65;">When the war ended, Texas had to answer a hard question. Who would hold power now, and what rights would the newly freed people have? You will see how Texas moved through Reconstruction, and why it ended the way it did.</div>
    </div>
    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#127919; BY THE END OF THIS LESSON YOU WILL BE ABLE TO&hellip;</div>
        <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px;">
            <div style="min-width: 26px; height: 26px; border-radius: 50%; background: #C7922C; color: #fff; font-size: 13px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0;">1</div>
            <div style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65;"><strong>I will be able to</strong> describe how freedom arrived in Texas in 1865 and what the <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A federal agency set up to help formerly enslaved people after the war.">Freedmen's Bureau</a> tried to do.</div>
        </div>
        <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px;">
            <div style="min-width: 26px; height: 26px; border-radius: 50%; background: #C7922C; color: #fff; font-size: 13px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0;">2</div>
            <div style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65;"><strong>I will be able to</strong> compare the first plan for <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="The years after the Civil War when the nation worked out how to bring the South back into the Union.">Reconstruction</a> with the plan Congress put in its place.</div>
        </div>
        <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px;">
            <div style="min-width: 26px; height: 26px; border-radius: 50%; background: #C7922C; color: #fff; font-size: 13px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0;">3</div>
            <div style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65;"><strong>I will be able to</strong> explain how Reconstruction ended in Texas in 1874, and what changed afterward.</div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128203; WHAT YOU NEED FOR THIS LESSON</div>
        <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 10px; margin-bottom: 8px; border-left: 3px solid #D0D9E8; padding-left: 14px;"><span style="color: #c7922c; font-size: 18px; flex-shrink: 0;">&#9654;</span>
            <div style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.6;">This page.</div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border-top: 3px solid #C7922C; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128218; PART 1 &middot; FREEDOM ARRIVES</div>
<h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">Juneteenth</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">On June 19, 1865, Union General Gordon Granger arrived at Galveston. He announced that all enslaved people in Texas were free. Texans still celebrate that day as Juneteenth. Black Texans began marking the anniversary the very next year, with picnics, prayers, and readings, and the custom spread across the country. Freedom had been law elsewhere for some time, but the Union army could not enforce it in Texas until the war was over.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">What did freedom bring? Many <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="People who had been enslaved and were now free.">freedpeople</a> left the farms where they had been enslaved. Some went to search for family members. Some went to the towns. Many wanted land and schools. They built churches and schools, and some started new communities of their own.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Many freedpeople hoped the government would give them land of their own. It did not. Most had no money to buy any, so they had to work for the people who owned the land. Black communities often built and paid for their own churches and schools, and teachers came to help from the North and from among freedpeople themselves.</p><h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">The Freedmen&rsquo;s Bureau and the Black Codes</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">The federal government set up the <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A federal agency set up to help formerly enslaved people after the war.">Freedmen's Bureau</a> to help. In Texas, its agents opened schools, helped freedpeople make fair work agreements, and tried to protect them from violence. But there were few agents and Texas was huge. Reports from agents describe beatings and killings of freedpeople that went unpunished.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">In 1866, the Texas legislature passed laws about work and about crime. One said that a person with no job could be arrested for &ldquo;vagrancy,&rdquo; and in some cases forced to work for a landowner. Freedpeople called such rules a trap.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Under the first plan for <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="The years after the Civil War when the nation worked out how to bring the South back into the Union.">Reconstruction</a>, set by President Andrew Johnson, Texas wrote a new state government in 1866. The legislature then passed <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="Laws passed in Southern states in 1865 and 1866 that limited the rights of freed people.">Black Codes</a>. These laws limited where freedpeople could work and what rights they had. Congress and many Northerners were angry, because they saw the Black Codes as slavery under another name.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border-top: 3px solid #C7922C; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128218; PART 2 &middot; TWO PLANS FOR RECONSTRUCTION</div>
<h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">Congress Takes Over</h3><div style="margin: 16px 0;"><div style="display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 10px;"><div style="min-width: 92px; font-size: 14px; color: #c7922c;">1865-1866</div><div style="flex: 1; background: #EEF1F8; border-left: 4px solid #0E1C42; border-radius: 6px; padding: 10px 14px;"><div style="font-size: 15px; color: #0E1C42; margin-bottom: 4px;"><strong>Presidential plan</strong></div><div style="font-size: 15px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.5;">President Johnson lets Southern whites rebuild their state governments quickly. Texas passes Black Codes.</div></div></div><div style="display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 10px;"><div style="min-width: 92px; font-size: 14px; color: #c7922c;">1867-1870</div><div style="flex: 1; background: #EEF1F8; border-left: 4px solid #0E1C42; border-radius: 6px; padding: 10px 14px;"><div style="font-size: 15px; color: #0E1C42; margin-bottom: 4px;"><strong>Congress takes over</strong></div><div style="font-size: 15px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.5;">The army governs Texas. Black men vote. A new constitution is written. Texas is let back into the Union.</div></div></div><div style="display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 10px;"><div style="min-width: 92px; font-size: 14px; color: #c7922c;">1874</div><div style="flex: 1; background: #EEF1F8; border-left: 4px solid #0E1C42; border-radius: 6px; padding: 10px 14px;"><div style="font-size: 15px; color: #0E1C42; margin-bottom: 4px;"><strong>Redemption</strong></div><div style="font-size: 15px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.5;">Democrats return to power. Governor Davis leaves office.</div></div></div><div style="font-size: 12px; color: #6b7a99;">The three stages of Reconstruction in Texas.</div></div><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">In 1867, Congress put a stricter plan in place. The army would govern Texas until the state met new rules. General Philip Sheridan removed Governor James Throckmorton from office. Black men were allowed to vote for the first time, and many white Texans who had led the Confederacy were not.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">To return to the Union, Texas had to approve the 13th, 14th, and 15th Amendments. They ended slavery, made Black Americans citizens, and protected the right of Black men to vote.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Texas wrote a new constitution in 1869. It gave Black men the vote and created a public school system. Black Texans were elected to office. George Ruby of Galveston and Matthew Gaines of Washington County served in the state senate. Edmund J. Davis, a Republican who had fought for the Union, became governor by a very narrow margin. In 1870, Texas was allowed back into the Union.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Davis tried to build a strong state government. He created a state police and a larger school system. His opponents hated both, and many said his government cost too much and held too much power.</p><div class="optima-widget ow-seq" data-ra-skip data-next="0" data-done="Each step changed who held power. The next question is who held it at each stage." data-hint="Not yet. Which of these came earlier?">
  <div class="widget-kicker">&#9889; TRY IT &middot; FROM FREEDOM TO READMISSION</div>
  <div class="widget-question">Freedom came in steps. Click these five steps in the order they happened.</div>
  <div class="seq-pool"><button type="button" class="seq-item" data-n="3" data-date="1869" data-text="Texas writes a new constitution" data-fb="It gave Black men the vote and created a public school system.">Texas writes a new constitution<span class="seq-tag">5. 1869</span></button><button type="button" class="seq-item" data-n="0" data-date="June 19, 1865" data-text="General Granger announces freedom in Galveston" data-fb="Texans still celebrate this day as Juneteenth.">General Granger announces freedom in Galveston<span class="seq-tag">5. June 19, 1865</span></button><button type="button" class="seq-item" data-n="4" data-date="1870" data-text="Texas is let back into the Union" data-fb="Edmund J. Davis was governor.">Texas is let back into the Union<span class="seq-tag">5. 1870</span></button><button type="button" class="seq-item" data-n="2" data-date="1867" data-text="Congress puts the army in charge of Texas" data-fb="Black men could vote for the first time.">Congress puts the army in charge of Texas<span class="seq-tag">5. 1867</span></button><button type="button" class="seq-item" data-n="1" data-date="1866" data-text="The legislature passes the Black Codes" data-fb="Many Northerners saw them as slavery under another name.">The legislature passes the Black Codes<span class="seq-tag">5. 1866</span></button></div>
  <div class="seq-msg"></div>
  <button class="widget-reset">Reset</button>
</div>
    </div>
    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border-top: 3px solid #C7922C; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128218; PART 3 &middot; THE END OF RECONSTRUCTION</div>
<h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">Redemption</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">White Texans who opposed Reconstruction began to win elections. Violence by groups like the Ku Klux Klan frightened many Republican voters and many Black voters. In 1872, Democrats won control of the legislature. In 1873, Richard Coke ran for governor against Davis and won by a wide margin.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Davis claimed the election was not legal. He asked President Ulysses S. Grant to send soldiers. Grant refused. In January 1874, Davis left office and Coke took his place. Democrats called their return to power <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="The name Democrats gave to their return to power in the South at the end of Reconstruction.">Redemption</a>. That word shows their view of it. Many freedpeople saw it as the loss of the rights they had just gained.</p><h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">What If Grant Had Sent Troops?</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Historians ask what might have happened if Grant had sent soldiers to support Davis in 1874. Soldiers might have kept Davis in office for a while, and that might have protected Black voters for longer. But Davis had lost the election by a wide margin, and many white Texans would have fought the army. Holding a government in place by force would have been very hard to keep up. Historians cannot be sure what would have happened.</p><h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">What Changed</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">The new Democratic government cut state spending, lowered taxes, and ended Davis&rsquo;s state police. It also weakened the public school system Davis had built. Texans soon wrote a new constitution that limited the power of government. Black Texans lost much of their political power over the following years. Many farmed as <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A farming system where a family works land owned by someone else and pays with part of the crop.">sharecropping</a> families on land owned by others. A landowner with little cash would let a family work a plot. The family got a place to live and supplies on credit, and paid back with a large share of the cotton crop. A bad year left them in debt, and the debt could last for years. At the same time, freedpeople kept the churches, schools, and communities they had built.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Reconstruction had not followed a straight path. It could have gone another way at several points, and the choices people made decided where it ended.</p><div class="optima-widget ow-select" data-ra-skip>
  <div class="widget-kicker">&#9889; TRY IT &middot; WHO HELD POWER?</div>
  <div class="widget-question">Reconstruction in Texas had three stages. For each row, decide which stage it belongs to.</div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="0" data-fb="Right. Under President Johnson’s plan, Southern whites rebuilt their governments quickly, and the Black Codes followed." data-hint="Think about who ran the state government before the army took over.">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">1. Texas passes laws about vagrancy and other Black Codes.</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">The presidential plan (1865 to 1866)</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">Congress takes over (1867 to 1870)</button><button type="button" data-choice="2">Redemption (1873 and 1874)</button></div>
    <div class="row-feedback ph" data-ph="Right. Under President Johnson’s plan, Southern whites rebuilt their governments quickly, and the Black Codes followed.">Right. Under President Johnson’s plan, Southern whites rebuilt their governments quickly, and the Black Codes followed.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="1" data-fb="Right. Congress’s stricter plan put the army in charge of Texas." data-hint="Which plan put the army in charge?">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">2. General Sheridan removes Governor Throckmorton from office.</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">The presidential plan (1865 to 1866)</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">Congress takes over (1867 to 1870)</button><button type="button" data-choice="2">Redemption (1873 and 1874)</button></div>
    <div class="row-feedback ph" data-ph="Right. Congress’s stricter plan put the army in charge of Texas.">Right. Congress’s stricter plan put the army in charge of Texas.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="2" data-fb="Right. The new Democratic government cut spending and undid much of what Davis had built." data-hint="Think about who took over after Davis left office.">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">3. The state police is ended and the school system is weakened.</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">The presidential plan (1865 to 1866)</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">Congress takes over (1867 to 1870)</button><button type="button" data-choice="2">Redemption (1873 and 1874)</button></div>
    <div class="row-feedback ph" data-ph="Right. The new Democratic government cut spending and undid much of what Davis had built.">Right. The new Democratic government cut spending and undid much of what Davis had built.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="1" data-fb="Right. This was one of the biggest changes Congress made." data-hint="Which stage let Black men vote for the first time?">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">4. Black men vote for the first time, and many Confederate leaders cannot.</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">The presidential plan (1865 to 1866)</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">Congress takes over (1867 to 1870)</button><button type="button" data-choice="2">Redemption (1873 and 1874)</button></div>
    <div class="row-feedback ph" data-ph="Right. This was one of the biggest changes Congress made.">Right. This was one of the biggest changes Congress made.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="2" data-fb="Right. Democrats called their return to power Redemption." data-hint="Whose return to power came with Coke’s win?">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">5. Richard Coke wins the race for governor.</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">The presidential plan (1865 to 1866)</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">Congress takes over (1867 to 1870)</button><button type="button" data-choice="2">Redemption (1873 and 1874)</button></div>
    <div class="row-feedback ph" data-ph="Right. Democrats called their return to power Redemption.">Right. Democrats called their return to power Redemption.</div>
  </div>
  <button class="widget-reset">Reset</button>
</div>
    </div>

    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border-top: 3px solid #C7922C; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 12px;">&#128170; WHAT TO CARRY FORWARD</div>
        <p style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0;">Freedom did not arrive all at once. It came in steps, and some of those steps were later taken back. As you read about Reconstruction, keep asking who had power at each stage, and what they chose to do with it.</p>
